course: world
lesson: 171
title: "I'm due a win": the gambler's fallacy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Jayden and his mate flip a coin for the last slice of pizza. It lands heads five times in a row. "Tails is DUE now, it has to be!" But the coin has no memory. On the next flip the chance of tails is still 1 in 2, exactly as it was on the first flip.
explain: Thinking a result is "due" because it hasn't come up for a while is called the gambler's fallacy. Dice don't save up sixes, lottery balls don't remember your numbers, scratch cards don't fill up a winning bar, and the weather doesn't take turns.
explain: In a game of skill it is different again: losing eight Mario Kart races in a row doesn't make you "due" a win; it usually means the other player is better. Winning four quizzes in a row usually means your team knows stuff. Practice changes your chances; a losing streak does not.
explain: Some games are made so most people lose: coconut shies are hard on purpose, some claw machines can be set to grip weakly, and lotteries pay back less than people spend. That is how they make money.
explain: The mickey-taking hits the "due" idea: "The coin doesn't know, bro. It's not keeping a diary. It's a coin."
fact: The gambler's fallacy is thinking a result is "due" because it hasn't happened for a while.
fact: A fair coin has a 1 in 2 chance of tails on every flip, whatever came before; over thousands of flips heads and tails roughly even out, but not because the coin "catches up".
fact: On a fair dice each number has a 1 in 6 chance on every roll.
fact: In the UK's main Lotto the chance of the jackpot is about 1 in 45 million for each ticket, every single draw, however long you have waited.
fact: Every set of six lottery numbers, including 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, is exactly as likely as any other set.
fact: In a game of skill, a long losing or winning streak usually tells you about skill, not about luck being "due".
fact: In rock-paper-scissors against a person, people have habits, and a good player can notice them.
fact: Fairground games and many claw machines are made so that most players lose; claw machines can be set by the owner to grip weakly most of the time.
fact: On average people get back less money from scratch cards and lotteries than they spend; that is how lotteries make money.
fact: Weather patterns can last for days or weeks; the best guide to next Saturday is the weather forecast.
fact: Before playing any game that costs money, decide your limit and stop when you reach it.
fact: Words people use: "mate" and "bro" mean friend, "lowkey" means a bit, "Nostradamus" is a joke name for someone who thinks they can tell the future (after a famous fortune-teller from long ago), "a rota" is a list of whose turn it is, "best of loads" means lots of rounds, and "candyfloss" is cotton candy (US).
exercise: A coin lands heads five times in a row. What is the chance of tails on the next flip? | 1 in 2, the same as always | Much more than 1 in 2 | Much less than 1 in 2 | Certain, tails is due
exercise: Why does the coin not "owe" Jayden a tails? | It has no memory of past flips | It is getting tired of landing heads | It only knows about pizza, not tails | It changes sides every five flips
exercise: Ella has lost eight Mario Kart races to her brother. What does that most likely show? | Her brother is better at the game | She is due a win next time | The game is broken | The controller in her hand is just unlucky for her
exercise: Leo misses the coconut ten times, and Dad says the odds are on his side now. What is true? | Each throw has about the same chance, and the game is hard on purpose | His chance rises with every miss | After ten misses a hit is certain | Coconuts get looser after ten throws
exercise: Mia needs a 6 and says sixes are "due". What is the chance of a 6 on her next roll? | 1 in 6 | 1 in 2 | 6 in 6 | 1 in 60
exercise: Kai's uncle's numbers haven't come up in 10 years. What is his chance this week? | About 1 in 45 million, the same as every week | Much better than in the first week | Certain, after 10 years | Worse, because the numbers are old now and the machine is bored of them
exercise: Freddie says 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 will never come up. What is true? | It is exactly as likely as any other set of numbers | It can never come up in a real lottery | It is less likely than birthday numbers | It is more likely than any other set, because it is so easy to remember
exercise: Arjun loses three times with rock, switches to paper, and his friend plays scissors. What was going on? | His friend was watching his habits | Rock was just unlucky for him that day | Paper is the weakest choice of the three | It was just pure luck every single time
exercise: A boy at the arcade says the claw machine is "building up" to a prize. What is true? | Some claw machines can be set to grip weakly most of the time | Claw machines must pay out after an hour | The prize gets a little easier to win with every single try you make | Claw machines are fair by law everywhere
exercise: Lily's team has won four quizzes and she says they're due a loss. They win again. Why? | They win because they know a lot, not because of luck | They were due a win, not a loss | The quiz was made easier for them | Losses always come after five wins in a row, so the next one will be a loss
exercise: Hannah's grandad says he must be close to a win on his scratch card. What is true? | Each card has the same chance as the last one | Each losing card brings his win a little bit closer | The cards remember who bought them and how often | After months and months of losing, a win is certain
exercise: Josh says next Saturday is bound to be sunny after five rainy Saturdays. What is the best guide? | The weather forecast | The last five Saturdays | Whose turn it is to have sun | A coin flip
exercise: What do grown-ups call thinking a result is "due" after a run without it? | The gambler's fallacy | A fair test | A hot streak | Moving the goalposts
exercise: Why do lotteries and fairground games make money? | On average players get back less than they spend | Players always win back their money | They are paid by the government | Only lucky people are allowed to play, so the rest of us pay for the prizes
exercise: What is a sensible rule before playing any game that costs money? | Decide your limit and stop when you reach it | Keep playing until you win back all your money | Play faster and faster whenever you start losing | Borrow some money if you feel a win coming on
test: His mate: "The coin doesn't know, bro. It's not keeping a diary. It's a coin." What is he mocking? | The idea that the coin remembers and must even out | Jayden for liking pizza | People who keep diaries | Coins for being boring
test: Brother: "Statistically you're due some practice, sis." What is he saying? | Losing doesn't make a win due; practice would help | She is due a win next race | She should stop playing forever | Statistics prove that the game is unfair and her brother is cheating
test: Mum: "The odds are on the coconut's side, Gary. The coconut is winning. Give the boy a candyfloss." What does "candyfloss" mean? | Cotton candy, a fluffy sweet at fairs | A type of coconut | A ticket for another throw | A fairground prize toy
test: Grandpa: "The dice has been saving up sixes for you, has it? Like pocket money?" What is the truth he is pointing at? | A fair dice has the same chance every roll | Dice really do save up their sixes for later | Sixes always come after lots of low numbers | His dice must be loaded so it never rolls sixes
test: Kai's aunt: "Bound to come up? The odds are millions to one, Dave. The only thing bound to come up is the bill." What does she mean? | The chance never improves, but the cost keeps adding up | The numbers will win next week | Dave should buy lots more tickets, so the numbers come up sooner | Bills are drawn like lottery balls
test: Sister: "And 7, 19, 23, 31, 40, 44 is more likely because it looks random? It's all numbers on balls." Which is true? | Both sets are exactly as likely | The random-looking set is more likely | The 1 to 6 set is more likely | Neither set can ever come up
test: Friend: "I'm not psychic, I just watched you. You do rock every time you're angry." What does this show? | Against a person, habits matter, not just luck | Rock is always the worst choice | His friend can read minds | Rock-paper-scissors is never fair, because some people can read minds
test: His mate: "It's not a piggy bank, it's a money-eating robot. Lowkey you just fed it lunch." What is the lesson? | Machines don't owe you a prize; set a limit first | Keep going, it must pay out soon | Feed it lunch money only on Fridays | Claw machines really are piggy banks, so you'll get it all back later
test: Teammate: "We're not due a loss, Lily, we just know stuff. Calm down, Nostradamus." Why "Nostradamus"? | She was acting as if she could see the future | It's the name of the quiz they were all playing | She is very good at history and old French names | It is just a fancy old way of saying "very clever"
test: His mum: "The clouds don't have a rota, love. Pack your raincoat." What does "the clouds don't have a rota" mean? | Weather doesn't take turns | Clouds come on Saturdays only | Clouds follow a timetable | It will be sunny next time
test: What would happen if you flipped a fair coin 10,000 times? | Heads and tails would come out roughly even, without any "catching up" | Heads and tails would come out exactly 5,000 each | Tails would always follow a run of heads | The coin would wear out and land heads
exam: Identify the principal error in the assertion that tails is "due" after five consecutive heads with a fair coin. | The coin retains no memory of prior outcomes | Five heads constitute an insufficient sample | The coin is likely to be weighted | Tails cannot occur after heads
exam: Ascertain the probability of obtaining a six upon a single roll of a fair die, irrespective of prior rolls. | One in six | One in two | One in thirty-six | Six in six
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the selection 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 in a lottery draw. | It is exactly as probable as any other selection of six numbers | It is impossible in any properly conducted draw | It is less probable than a random-appearing selection | It is more probable than any other selection
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the principal means by which lotteries derive revenue? | Players receive back, on average, less than they expend | Players invariably recover their stake | The draw is adjusted to favour regular players | The government guarantees each player's return and recovers it through taxation
